course: everyday
lesson: 125
title: More greetings and making introductions
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many families greet in their own way: "Namaste" with palms pressed together is a respectful Indian and Nepali greeting, and "As-salamu alaykum", peace be upon you, is a Muslim greeting answered with "Wa alaykum as-salam" or simply hello.
explain: To introduce yourself, you say your name and maybe one thing about you: "Hi, I'm Sam, I'm in Year 5 and I love football"; people find it easier to talk once they know your name.
explain: To introduce a friend to an older person, the old custom is to say the older person's name first: "Grandma, this is Tom."
explain: Online it is different: in a game you never share your full name, address, school or photo, because strangers online may not be who they say they are.
explain: A girl in Vancouver meets her dad's friend who uses a wheelchair; she greets him just as she greets anyone, and during the long chat she sits down so they are at the same eye level.
fact: "Namaste" with palms pressed together is a respectful Indian and Nepali greeting.
fact: "As-salamu alaykum" is a Muslim greeting meaning peace be upon you, and the reply is "Wa alaykum as-salam".
fact: To introduce yourself, you say your name and maybe one or two things about you.
fact: When introducing a younger person to an older one, you say the older person's name first.
fact: When someone introduces themselves, you answer "Nice to meet you, I'm ..." to finish the introduction.
fact: In front of the class you stand up, speak clearly and say your name and one or two things about you.
fact: If someone's name is said wrongly, you gently give the right name or let the person correct it.
fact: Words like "This is my stepdad, Mark" help others understand who people are.
fact: In a new club or team, introducing yourself to the coach and to one or two children helps you settle in.
fact: Online you use a nickname and never share your full name, address, school or photo.
fact: A person in a wheelchair is greeted the same way as anyone, at their eye level if the talk is long.
exercise: Someone greets you with "Namaste" and palms pressed together. What can you do? | Press your palms together and say "Namaste", or say hello | Shake their hand very hard | Ask them to say hello in English instead | Say nothing at all because you do not know the word or what it means
exercise: Someone says "As-salamu alaykum". What can you reply? | "Wa alaykum as-salam", or simply hello | "Good night" | Nothing, because it is not meant for you | "Namaste" with a bow
exercise: How do you introduce yourself to someone new? | "Hi, I'm Sam", and maybe something about you | Wait for them to guess your name | Tell them your full address and phone number first | Say "Who are you?"
exercise: You introduce your friend Tom to your grandma. What do you say? | "Grandma, this is Tom" | "Tom, here is some old lady" | "This is Grandma, Tom, whatever" | Nothing, they can introduce themselves
exercise: Someone says "Hi, I'm Priya." How do you reply? | "Nice to meet you, I'm ..." and smile | "Okay" and look away | "I know" and walk off | "That is a strange name, where did your parents get it?"
exercise: The teacher asks you to introduce yourself to the class. What is best? | Stand up, speak clearly, say your name and one or two things about you | Mumble your name while looking at the floor | Tell a long story about your whole summer holiday, day by day, every detail | Refuse, since the teacher already knows you
exercise: Someone calls your new classmate "Lee" but his name is "Li". What do you do? | Gently say the right name, or let him correct it | Laugh because the mistake is funny | Start calling him "Lee" too, since that is easier for everyone | Tell everyone he has a silly name
exercise: How can you introduce your stepfather to a friend? | "This is my stepdad, Mark" | "This is some man who lives with us" | "This is Mark" and say nothing more ever | Avoid introducing him at all
exercise: You join a football club for the first time. What helps you settle in? | Introduce yourself to the coach and to one or two children | Wait silently until someone asks your name | Only talk to the coach for the whole season | Tell everyone you were the best player at your old club, by far
exercise: A player in an online game asks your real name and school. What do you do? | Do not share them and use your nickname | Tell them, because they seem friendly | Share only your school and your street, but not your full name | Send a photo instead of your name
exercise: You are introduced to a man using a wheelchair. How do you greet him? | The same way as anyone, at his eye level if the talk is long | Talk only to the person pushing the wheelchair, not to the man himself | Speak very slowly and loudly to him | Greet the wheelchair before the man
exercise: Why do we answer an introduction with our own name? | It finishes the introduction so both people know each other | Because people need to know your name before they can shake your hand | Because the other person will forget theirs | Because it is rude to say "Nice to meet you"
test: A boy in Birmingham meets his friend's grandmother, who says "As-salamu alaykum". What is a kind reply? | "Wa alaykum as-salam", or hello | "Goodbye, see you again tomorrow" | A silent stare | "Kia ora, good night"
test: A girl in Florida wants to introduce her friend Max to her old neighbour Mrs Lee. What is the traditional way? | "Mrs Lee, this is Max" | "Max, this is Mrs Lee, she is old" | "Hey, you two, talk to each other" | "Mrs Lee, you must know Max already"
test: In a new chess club, nobody speaks to you for ten minutes. What is the best step? | Introduce yourself to the coach and one or two players | Leave and never come back | Wait until next month, when they know you, before saying anything | Tell them they are unfriendly
test: Someone in a game chat says "I'm 10 too! Which school do you go to?" What is safe? | Keep your school private and tell a parent if they keep asking | Tell them the school, because they are the same age as you so it's safe | Give the name of your street instead | Send a selfie so they believe you
test: Why should you sit down when chatting for a long time with someone in a wheelchair? | So you talk at the same eye level, as you would with anyone | Because standing too close to a wheelchair is not allowed by the rules | Because they cannot hear people who stand | Because it is the only way to see the wheels
test: An Indian family friend greets you with palms together. Which answer is fine? | Palms together and "Namaste", or a friendly hello | A fist bump straight into their palms | Turning away, since you do not know the custom and might get it wrong | A bow from the waist like in Japan
test: Your teacher keeps saying the new girl's name wrongly and she looks unhappy. What could you do? | Gently tell the teacher the right way to say it, or help her to say it | Say it wrongly too, so that the girl slowly gets used to the new way of saying it | Tell the girl to change her name | Laugh with the class
test: You have to introduce yourself at a new Scout group. Which is best? | "Hi, I'm Ava, I'm nine and I like camping" | "Hi" and nothing more for the rest of the evening | Your full address and phone number | A story about everything you did last year
test: new kid says hi im sam, wat do i say back | Nice to meet you, I'm ... and your name | Just nod and look away | Ask what his surname is first, so you know who he really is | Say "I know" and walk on
test: playin online, game asks my full name and school, put it in? | No, use a nickname and keep those private | Yes, games need real details | Yes, but only your school | Yes, if the game is popular and lots of your friends play it
test: introducing my lil cousin to grandad, who's name goes first | Grandad's, the older person first | Your cousin's, the younger first | It doesn't matter at all | Your own name first
test: my mate reckons namaste is just a yoga word, right? | No, it's a respectful Indian and Nepali greeting | Yes, it's only ever said at the start and end of yoga classes | It means goodbye in Japan | It's a type of food
explain: A boy in Ohio told an online gamer his full name, school and age when the gamer introduced himself; the gamer, an adult, began messaging him at school times, and his parents had to report it.
explain: A girl in Leeds did not introduce her friend to her nan; her friend felt left out and sat silently all afternoon.
fact: Introducing yourself online is different: you never share your full name, school, address or age with someone you do not know in real life.
fact: Introducing people to each other helps everyone feel included.
test: new player wants my name, school n age to be friends | Don't share, tell a trusted adult | Share, he's your friend now | Give your school only | Ask him first then share
test: my mate came over n i forgot to introduce her to my nan | Introduce them now, it helps her feel welcome | Leave it, they'll work it out | Ask your friend to leave | Tell nan she's nobody
test: someone online says they're 10 and asks where i live | Don't tell, people online may not be who they say | Tell them, kids are safe | Tell only your town | Send a photo of your house instead, so you don't have to type it
test: did u hear, the new girl's cousin told some gamer his school n now the police are at his house every day apparently | Never share real details online; the police part is probably blown up | Share your school only with gamers who seem nice and say they are your age | The story proves gaming is always illegal | Police visit every child who plays online games
test: my mate reckons the new kid said "namaste" to the teacher and everyone laughed, he's so weird lol, pass it on? | Don't pass it on; namaste is a respectful greeting, laughing at it is unkind | Pass it on, it's a funny story | Tell the new kid to stop greeting people like that, so that they stop laughing | Only pass it on to your best friends
test: apparently that boy in year 6 never says nice to meet you and his nan's dead embarrassed, my mum's mate saw it | It may be just gossip; the real lesson is to answer an introduction kindly | It's surely true, because one of the mums' friends saw it happen with her own eyes | Nobody needs to answer an introduction at all | Go and tell the boy's nan what people say
exam: Identify the customary order of names when a younger person is introduced to an older person. | The older person's name is stated first | The younger person's name is stated first | The names are stated in alphabetical order | Only the younger person's name is stated
exam: Which of the following constitutes an appropriate response when an individual introduces himself or herself to you? | "Nice to meet you, I'm ..." followed by one's own name | A silent nod without any further words | A request for the person's home address | An immediate account of one's own whole day
exam: Pursuant to sound practice in respect of online games, which particulars may be disclosed to an unknown player? | Only a nickname, never one's real name or school | One's full name, provided the player seems kind | One's school and age, but not one's address | Any detail, provided it is sent in private
exam: Select the response consistent with established courtesy towards a person who uses a wheelchair during a lengthy conversation. | Greet them as anyone, and sit at their eye level | Speak only to the person pushing the wheelchair | Raise one's voice so as to be clearly heard | Avoid greeting them so as not to cause trouble
